Whenever she made this sort of random comment, it only made him love her more. It gave him a warm, fuzzy feeling inside. Even so, he wasn’t sure how to respond. “Sure,” he finally said, grinning. “I’ve grown tall enough to be your husband.”
She gasped playfully at this sly comment. “Do you like how I look?” he asked.
“Well… hard to say.” Flustered, she started walking ahead with stiff steps. Seeing her acting shy like that made him smile even more warmly.
On their way back, he told her about his beautiful dream, which would soon become reality; their house, the life they would share together there, their beautiful children, and the new chapter in the history of the Ettman family that they and their children would write. He noticed with gladness that she was listening with what looked to be tentative anticipation. He wanted to turn

that anticipation into reality. He even went as far as to wonder whether doing so was the reason he had been put on this earth.
“How many kids should we have?” he asked. “Personally, I’d like it if we have a daughter and a son.”
“Hmm… I think five would be about right.”
He was shocked by the number she named, which seemed to be quite at odds with the hesitant way she usually discussed their future life together.
Smiling brightly, she went on. “If we have a lot of kids like that, none of them will be lonely. And they’ll be able to rely on each other for help when they need it. I love the thought of our home filled with a bunch of faces that resemble each other.”
Despite the joyful look on her face, he felt a pang in his chest. Her bright smile only made him realize how lonely a life she had led. “Five kids…? All right, Layla. I’ll do my best!”
“Huh?” Her eyes widened at his bold reply.
Staring at each other, they both started to blush at almost the same instant. “I w-wasn’t talking about… that,” he said, taking a step back. He was
blushing more brightly than she was. “That’s not all I think about, you know!” “Me neither!” she replied.
Both of them felt too embarrassed to make eye contact. They stopped holding hands, and started walking with a bit of distance between them.
Soon, stealing a sidelong glance at her, he burst into laughter, saying, “But Layla, if you’re too shy for this sort of talk, how do you expect us to make five babies?”
With a gasp, she turned and glared at him.
“Miss Llewellyn, I know you excel at all subjects other than geometry. You didn’t expect a stork to come with five babies in its beak, did you?”

Now that he was teasing her even more, she started walking faster, still frowning. His delighted laughter grew louder along with the accelerating sound of her heels clicking on the ground. “Wait up, Mrs. Ettman!” he playfully shouted. His voice reverberated throughout the campus.
She started to run. Her braided ponytail bounced and swung behind her.
He relished the sight as he watched her from behind. He felt like he was back in that wonderful dream. He didn’t want to wake up. Ever.




31.

Kind Yet Cold-hearted.






Is it because that last coin didn’t go in?
When Layla heard that Duke Herhardt would be returning to Arvis, the first thing she thought of was the last throw she had made at the fountain, when the coin had missed the basin.
She was certain she had heard that the duke was going to spend another year serving in the imperial guard, and yet now she was learning that this wasn’t the case anymore. Instead, he was going to tie up any loose ends in the capital, then move back to Arvis where he would live fulltime. The elderly Dowager Herhardt wanted her grandson to live with her, looking after the estate, and it seemed that he could no longer ignore her wish.
Well, I don’t care, Layla thought as she stretched her arms before tying her apron tightly around her waist. His life is no longer of any concern to me. I’m just

a little upset that that last coin he gave me missed the basin after I wished that I would never see him again.
After leaving Arvis the previous summer, he had not visited at all for an entire year. She had assumed this pattern would continue until his military service was complete. As long as she moved away from Arvis before then, her wish would have come true.
Shaking her head, she stepped out of the cottage into the backyard. Maybe my wish will come true anyway. The duke is returning, but I’m moving away from here.
Breathing a sigh of relief, she tended to the animals and the vegetable garden. Just as she was finishing up her morning chores, her pigeon Phoebe returned from the Ettmans’ house. A note from Kyle was wrapped around her ankle: My mom wants to invite you to dinner tonight. See, Layla? I was right!
She felt like she could hear Kyle’s voice behind the words of the note.
To calm Layla’s concerns, he had guaranteed that his mother was going to come around to liking her eventually. He had assured her that everything was going to turn out all right, that by the fall they would be a married couple strolling the university campus together. They would be happy, very happy.
