I can’t believe that my son is going to marry a girl like that, Mrs. Ettman thought, clenching her fists under the table. She wasn’t happy with the situation, but she had decided to stop resisting. She knew her husband and son well enough to realize that too much resistance could backfire. She took a deep breath to regain her composure, then looked at Layla with even more geniality and said, “I hope you enjoy your meal, Layla. I made sure to include lots of dishes that you like.”
Layla, who had been staring at the full table in amazement, smiled when she heard that the food had been planned with her in mind.
Looking at Layla’s pretty face, Mrs. Ettman started to feel a strange resentment bubbling up inside of her. You used that pretty smile to seduce my Son, didn’t you? Realizing with a chill the extreme to which her negative thoughts had reached, she quickly lifted her water glass to her lips. The water felt extremely cold going down her throat.
“Thank you so much,” Layla said with utter sincerity before flashing another polite smile.
She was a kind, well-behaved girl. Mrs. Ettman was well aware of this fact, and it had become yet another reason why she didn’t look forward to having her as a daughter-in-law.
She would have greatly preferred it if there were some substantial problem with her, some excuse to dislike or even hate her. Then at least she wouldn’t feel so much shame for disliking her in spite of all her virtues.

In fact, she disliked Layla for her virtues. She hated that she was so nice, she hated that she was so smart, and she hated that she came from such pitiful circumstances.
Noticing that the smile was fading from his wife’s face, Dr. Ettman decided to change the subject. “Layla, there’s a professor at Ratse University named Dr. Lorenz who’s an expert in avian biology. Make sure to sign up for a class with him when the school year starts. I think it could be quite beneficial for you to study under a great scholar like him.”
