Layla, sitting across the table from Mrs. Ettman, smiled bashfully at the compliment.
When it came to Layla’s early life in Lovita, Bill never told anyone anything. Other residents of Arvis knew only that as a young girl she had lost her parents and been passed around between relatives in Lovita, before finally being sent to Berg.
Linda Ettman hated this about her more than anything, the fact that she had no proper relatives to raise her and care for her, and the fact that she was kicked out of home after home, before finally being sent to a foreign country.

She almost shuddered when she thought of what a bad foundation that would be for her when starting a new family with Kyle.
It’s not like I’m asking for much, Mrs. Ettman thought.
If Layla had simply come from an ordinary family, Mrs. Ettman wouldn’t have been so vehemently opposed to her marrying her son. But given Layla’s lack of a stable family growing up, she worried that she would be unable to become a good wife or mother. She also looked down on what she saw as Layla’s greedy desire to go to college. The way Mrs. Ettman saw it, if Layla wanted this, despite being both female and poor, then she was likely greedy in all sorts of other ways. She thus highly doubted that her son could be happy with her as his wife.
I’ve got to stop it, Mrs. Ettman thought, clasping her hands together under the table. I’ve got to stop this marriage from happening, no matter what it takes.







Layla’s college acceptance letter was delivered by the very same mailman who had delivered Layla herself to Arvis several years earlier. Layla was out on a walk when the letter arrived, so Bill received it on her behalf. As soon as he saw the envelope, he went silent and started staring into space.
